8493883a6562"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For your linear model, state how you created the features you \
extracted/generated and how they were calculated.\nAlso, state how you split \
the data up for your models.\ni.e. display a sample of the data you used for \
your models and how it was generated (you may not be using all features in \
both models).\n",
 StyleBox["Answer:\nFeature Extraction and Generation",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0., 0., 1.]]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.840607725384644*^9, 3.840607760872209*^9}, {
   3.8406078022778606`*^9, 3.8406078102046776`*^9}, {3.8406106963508224`*^9, 
   3.8406107088405824`*^9}, {3.8406109923753786`*^9, 3.840611013098338*^9}, {
   3.8406110693175955`*^9, 3.8406111194349594`*^9}, {3.840619531338698*^9, 
   3.8406196000348096`*^9}, {3.840620019754721*^9, 3.8406200231767826`*^9}, {
   3.840620153516815*^9, 3.8406201535701885`*^9}, {3.840623483957057*^9, 
   3.8406235347469196`*^9}, 3.840624054372602*^9, {3.847435872938201*^9, 
   3.847435879594899*^9}, {3.8474359475579014`*^9, 3.8474360591044674`*^9}, {
   3.8474361740509377`*^9, 3.8474361776059756`*^9}, {3.8782095795412474`*^9, 
   3.8782096051319947`*^9}, {3.9681335546263337`*^9, 
   3.9681335601243925`*^9}, {4.0038413814585967`*^9, 
   4.0038413877946033`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a9464fb5-ad04-4829-9532-\
d294afe832c4"],

Cell["\<\
Biases was prepended to every row
Then we extracted all of the numerical rows for the data. With the exception \
of \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Qual\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] rows from the data \
that represent a constant number of quality values, such as \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Gd\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Ta\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Po\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
etc. We mapped this finite amount of unique strings to values 5-0.
We made a simpler version of the bathrooms rows, to a single TotalBathroom \
per household. We added Fullbath +0.5(HalfBath)+BsmtFullBath+0.5(BsmtHalfBath).
We did a similar thing to the square footage values. Adding \
TotalBsmtSF+1stFlrSF+2ndFlrSF
Depending on the row, we would replace missing or NA data with the mean or a \
value of 0 (in the case of the extracted features above)
We also implemented feature scaling to the values of all rows using Z score \
normalisation. Without this the gradient decent would have imbalanced figures \
for the likes of lotArea (in the tens of thousands) compared to a value like \
total bathrooms (usually below 10)
The data set is pre split into a training file along with a test document \
that we preserved when training.
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8406077657661633`*^9, 3.8406077988842287`*^9}, {
  4.003841336774948*^9, 4.003841352934289*^9}, {4.0038425637443485`*^9, 
  4.003843153050489*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"44107e52-a06b-4860-a993-\
377a7a919e2e"],

Cell["Code used to produce answer:", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8406212671583977`*^9, 3.840621294907153*^9}, {
  3.840623756059304*^9, 3.840623756233817*^9}},
 FontWeight->"Bold",
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0., 0., 1.],ExpressionUUID->"0f04cc29-7d41-4258-98d1-3db09eba00fb"]
}, Open  ]],

